<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hromkla</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rumkale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hromkla, Turkish Rumkale, is a fortress on a crag above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. From 1149 it was the seat of the Catholicos of the Armenian Church and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript-illumination">manuscript illumination</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toros-roslin">Toros Roslin</a> worked. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> captured it in 1292, and the catholicosate moved to Sis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hromkla.jpg" alt="Hromkla" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from Frankfurt, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Euphrates_River_in_Turkey,_Rumkale_(52031477798).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rumkale, Roman Castle, stands on a cliff above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in Gaziantep Province, 50 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanliurfa">Şanlıurfa</a>. It has been suggested to be Shitamrat, taken by the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> in 855 BCE, and tradition holds that the apostle John lived there, but no remains older than about 1000 CE have been found.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The foundations go back to Byzantine rule, when the fortress guarded the frontier and the Roman road along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and housed a Syriac Orthodox bishopric in the 5th and 6th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> settled there in the 11th century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071 it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos Brachamios</a> and then to Kogh Vasil, whose adopted son Vasil Dgha was tortured by Baldwin II of Edessa and forced to give up his lands in 1116. Catholicos Gregory III bought it from Beatrice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turbessel">Turbessel</a> as a safe home for the Armenian Catholicosate, and his brother Nerses IV was elected Catholicos there in 1166.</p><p>Theological conferences on a union of the Armenian and Byzantine churches met there in 1170 and 1172, and a synod of 33 Armenian bishops in 1179 sent a profession of faith to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a>, who died before it arrived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> forces destroyed the town in 1268 but could not take the citadel, and a siege under Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a> in 1280 also failed. In 1292 the Mamluks under al-Ashraf took it after a long siege, massacred the garrison and enslaved or imprisoned the clergy and townspeople. Renamed Qal'at al-Muslimin, it became a base for raids into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s forces damaged the castle in 1400–1, and in 1516 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> without a siege after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marj-dabiq">Battle of Marj Dabiq</a>. After the rebellion of its voivode Bekirzade Mehmed Bey in 1831 the Ottoman government ordered the town emptied and its houses destroyed, and in 1832 Ibrahim Pasha bombarded what remained during the Egyptian–Ottoman War. The residents moved to Kasaba, Halfeti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaziantep">Aintab</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The fortress is a ruin in the Nizip district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaziantep">Gaziantep</a>. The reservoir of the Birecik Dam has made its site a peninsula, reached by boat from the neighbouring site of Zeugma or from the town of Halfeti.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rumkale” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church ran through the land: the Catholicos, assisted by fourteen bishops under Levon I, sat at <b>Hromkla</b> from 1149 and at Sis after 1293, with up to sixty monasteries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
